{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"lost-random-raccoon","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# S2 — honour `Retry-After` on 429, scoped to IDEMPOTENT READS\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nSplit out of [rebar:b586-2fda-8a15-4260] (ten BLOCK rounds; full record on that ticket). S2 is the\nPOLICY half; [rebar:f2f3-9cb1-335b-4e31] (S1) is the mechanical relocation that makes room for it\nand **must land first** — `transport.py` sits at 789/800 lines, so this code cannot be added in\nplace.\n\nJira Data Center has built-in rate limiting (8.6+, **DC only**, **OFF BY DEFAULT** — an admin\nenables it), a per-user token bucket. rebar's DC transport does not handle it: `_with_connection_retry`\nretries only connection-level faults and its docstring states that `jira.exceptions.JIRAError` —\nany HTTP 4xx/5xx — is NOT in the retry set, so a 429 propagates as a hard failure on the first\noccurrence, on an instance that was merely asking rebar to slow down.\n\nTwo constraints shape the whole fix:\n1. **A static pacing policy is NOT implementable.** The limit is admin-configured and may be absent\n   entirely (pre-8.6, Server, or simply disabled), so honour headers WHEN PRESENT and degrade\n   gracefully when absent.\n2. **Atlassian prescribes `Retry-After` PLUS up to 20% jitter**, which differs from ADR-0036's\n   backoff curve (designed for transient connectivity, where the server offers no guidance).\n   Jitter is not decoration: every rebar instance hitting the same bucket would otherwise retry in\n   lockstep.\n\n**VERIFY THE NUMBERS BEFORE BUILDING TO THEM** — \"off by default\", \"8.6+\" and \"retry-after + 20%\njitter\" come from OSS research with Atlassian citations, not from testing, and that research\nretracted two of its other claims on a second pass.\n\n## Approach\n\n**Reuse what exists — do not build a third `Retry-After` implementation.** `_errors.parse_retry_after()`\nand `_errors.MAX_BACKOFF_S` already exist, `dispatch_one.py:41` already imports both, and\n`dispatch_one.py:150-171` is ALREADY a 429-honouring retry path for the REST transport. Read that\npath first and mirror it; a second parser is the finding that opened round 1.\n\n**SCOPE THE RETRY TO IDEMPOTENT READS. This is the load-bearing constraint.**\n`_with_connection_retry` is the single choke point for ALL 15 transport call sites INCLUDING\n`create_issue`, `add_comment` and `add_label`, and its docstring already records why JIRAError is\nunretried: \"retrying a mutation on an HTTP error risks duplicates\". Retrying 429s there would\n**reintroduce the duplicate-issue class [rebar:21fc-51d7-90ca-4a03] just fixed** — a 429 can arrive\nafter the server began the write, and nothing in the response distinguishes that from rejection at\nthe gate. So: per-CALL opt-in, **defaulting to OFF**, so a mutation added later cannot inherit the\nretry by omission.\n\n**WHAT ESCAPES WHEN THE CAP IS EXHAUSTED — the design hole round 10 flagged and left open.**\n`BackendHTTPError` with `code == 429`, never the raw `JIRAError`. This is not a preference: it is\nwhat the boundary already does. `_with_connection_retry` catches the library error types and raises\n`_as_backend_http_error(exc) from exc`, which reads `.status_code` into `BackendHTTPError.code`\nusing urllib's spelling SO THAT the core's existing `except urllib.error.HTTPError` branches\nclassify a DC failure exactly as they classify a Cloud one. Leaking `JIRAError` past that boundary\nis the actual regression risk here, and J10 pinned the neutral exception contract precisely to stop\nit. (A library error with no usable status degrades to `code=0`, which no core branch mistakes for a\n404 or a success.)\n\n## Scope\n\n- `adapters/jira_datacenter/retry.py` (created by S1) — the 429 branch, jitter-then-clamp, and the\n  per-call opt-in parameter.\n- the read call sites that opt in — enumerated in Testing below, not gestured at.\n\n**NOT in scope:** any mutation call site, and any change to ADR-0036's connectivity backoff curve.\n\n## Testing\n\n* **the cap-exhaustion contract** — a client that returns 429 forever: assert the escaping\n  exception is `BackendHTTPError` with `code == 429`, AND assert `jira.exceptions.JIRAError` does\n  NOT escape. A test that only checks \"it raised\" passes while the vendor type leaks.\n* **`Retry-After` is honoured when present** — the sleep is driven by the header, not the ADR-0036\n  curve; jitter is applied and the result is CLAMPED to `MAX_BACKOFF_S`.\n* **absent header degrades gracefully** — no header (limiter off / pre-8.6 / Server) behaves exactly\n  as today.\n* **MUTATIONS DO NOT RETRY, enumerated rather than sampled** — assert one 429 fails immediately for\n  EVERY non-opting mutation member of the transport. Enumerate them explicitly; an ellipsis is how\n  a later-added mutation silently inherits the retry.\n* **the opt-in defaults to OFF** — a call that does not pass the flag does not retry a 429.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] [rebar:f2f3-9cb1-335b-4e31] (S1) has LANDED before this story starts — `transport.py` has no\n      room otherwise, and building on an unextracted module is what interleaved b586's rounds\n- [ ] a 429 with a `Retry-After` header is retried on an opted-in READ, with jitter applied and the\n      delay clamped to `MAX_BACKOFF_S`, asserted numerically\n- [ ] `_errors.parse_retry_after` and `_errors.MAX_BACKOFF_S` are REUSED — a test or the diff shows\n      no second parser and no second constant\n- [ ] cap exhaustion raises `BackendHTTPError` with `code == 429`, and a test asserts `JIRAError`\n      does not escape the adapter boundary\n- [ ] EVERY mutation call site still fails on the FIRST 429 — asserted per member over an explicit\n      enumeration of the transport's mutation members, not a sample\n- [ ] the opt-in parameter defaults to OFF, asserted, so a future call inherits no retry by omission\n- [ ] how `_paged_search` opts in is stated unambiguously — either it always passes the flag, or it\n      accepts and forwards one from its callers; round 10 flagged these as opposite failure modes.\n      Note `_paged_search` DOES route through `_call_logged` (verified on `origin/main`\n      post-9263: defined at `transport.py:421`, called from the project reads and the parent-map read)\n- [ ] the Scope's count of opted-in read call sites MATCHES the enumeration in Testing — round 10\n      found \"ELEVEN\" in prose against TEN table rows, which was an arithmetic slip, not a design gap\n- [ ] the three researched numbers (DC-only, 8.6+, off-by-default; retry-after + up to 20% jitter)\n      are each either confirmed against the target DC version or explicitly marked unverified","id":"6758-26b8-c9ea-4c5d","parent_id":"b586-2fda-8a15-4260","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"S2 — honour Retry-After on 429, scoped to idempotent reads"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785615643858007001,"uuid":"1fa10091-9e01-47e6-a2a7-334c79d0807c"}